'''Ancient Roar''' (Japanese: '''{{ruby|古代|こだい}}の{{ruby|咆哮|ほうこう}}''') and '''Future Flash''' (Japanese: ''{{ruby|未来|みらい}}の{{ruby|一閃|いっせん}}'') are the names given to the two Japanese paired sets that collectively make up the fourth expansion of cards released during the [[Pokémon Trading Card Game]]'s {{TCG|Scarlet & Violet Era}}.

==Information==
'''Ancient Roar''' and '''Future Flash''' feature [[Paradox Pokémon]]. Although {{p|Koraidon}} and {{p|Great Tusk}} appeared in the {{TCG|Scarlet ex}} expansion and {{p|Miraidon}} and {{p|Iron Treads}} appeared in the {{TCG|Violet ex}} expansion, Ancient Roar and Future Flash feature Paradox Pokémon that do not appear outside of [[Area Zero]] in the [[Pokémon Scarlet and Violet]] video games. Card illustrations from these sets depict Pokémon within and around Area Zero.

Two new variants of cards are introduced in these expansions: '''{{TCG|Ancient}}''' cards are available in Ancient Roar, while '''{{TCG|Future}}''' cards are available in Future Flash. These variants are available as both Pokémon and Trainer cards. Both card variants feature unique text icons on the top right corner of the artwork, and unique backgrounds adorning the right half of the cards: Ancient cards have a layered {{wp|Stratum|strata}} background, while Future cards have a pixelated background. Ancient Pokémon cards tend to have direct attacks that deal a large amount of damage and are difficult to Knock Out. Future Pokémon cards tend to have {{TCG|Ability|Abilities}} and attacks with extra effects, allowing for craftier strategies. {{TCG ID|Paradox Rift|Ancient Booster Energy Capsule|159}} can bolster Ancient Pokémon defensively and enable {{TCG ID|Paradox Rift|Brute Bonnet|123}}'s Ability, while {{TCG ID|Paradox Rift|Future Booster Energy Capsule|164}} can empower Future Pokémon's attacks and reduce the cost of {{TCG ID|Paradox Rift|Iron Jugulis|216}}'s second attack.

Additional strategies are available to other Pokémon in the set. {{TCG ID|Paradox Rift|Tsareena ex|46}} can add damage counters to an opponent's Pokémon and reduce their remaining HP below 30, which prevents that Pokémon from attacking while {{TCG ID|Paradox Rift|Vanilluxe|45}} is in play. The [[treasures of ruin]] {{TCG ID|Paradox Rift|Wo-Chien|18}}, {{TCG ID|Paradox Rift|Chi-Yu|29}}, {{TCG ID|Paradox Rift|Chien-Pao|57}}, and {{TCG ID|Paradox Rift|Ting-Lu|109}} each have attacks that can each accelerate Energy from the discard pile to the player's Benched Pokémon. {{TCG ID|Paradox Rift|Simisage|5}}, {{TCG ID|Paradox Rift|Simisear|21}}, and {{TCG ID|Paradox Rift|Simipour|42}} each have the {{cw|Monkey Trio}} Ability, which reduces their attack costs while all three are in play.

There are no {{ct|Lightning}} or {{ct|Metal}} Pokémon in Ancient Roar. Likewise, there are no {{ct|Darkness}} Pokémon in Future Flash.

Each set contains five {{TCG|Pokémon ex}}, one Tera Pokémon ex, 12 {{TCG|illustration rare}} Pokémon, nine {{TCG|ultra rare}} full-art etched Pokémon ex and Supporter cards, five {{TCG|special illustration rare}} Pokémon and Supporter cards, and three {{TCG|hyper rare}} gold etched cards.

Ancient Roar and Future Flash were released in Japanese on October 27, 2023, followed by localized releases in Traditional Chinese on November 10, 2023, and in Thai on December 15, 2023. The Indonesian set equivalent, {{ATCG|Paradox Encounters}}, also released on December 15, 2023.

Cards released in this sets were included primarily in the English {{TCG|Paradox Rift}} expansion. The Hyper rare prints of {{TCG|Basic Darkness Energy}} and {{TCG|Basic Metal Energy}} from Ancient Roar and Future Flash respectively are instead included in {{TCG|Shrouded Fable}}. Other cards from these sets not included in Paradox Rift are included in the {{TCG|Paldean Fates}} expansion.

