'''Pokémon TCG: ''Scarlet & Violet—Obsidian Flames''''' is the third main expansion of cards from the Scarlet & Violet Series of the [[Pokémon Trading Card Game]]. It was released on August 11, 2023.

==Blurb==
<i>Raging Flames Forged in Darkness!

Red-hot embers illuminate the pitch-black night and sparks flare into an inferno as Charizard ex surges forth with newfound powers of darkness! The glittering Terastal phenomenon imbues some Pokémon ex like Tyranitar, Eiscue, and Vespiquen with different types than usual, while Dragonite ex and Greedent ex show mastery of their own inner strengths. Not to be outdone, Revavroom ex, Melmetal ex, and more Pokémon promise to change the course of battle in the Scarlet & Violet—Obsidian Flames expansion!</i>

==Information==
'''Obsidian Flames'''  is the first set to feature Tera {{TCG|Pokémon ex}} with {{TCG|type}}s that differ from their usual types. Type-shifted Tera Pokémon ex use the same Energy types as their usual type for their attacks, but have differing Weakness and Resistance in accordance with their shifted type. Other cards from this set depict [[Pokémon Center]]s in the [[Paldea]] [[region]] in the illustrations' backgrounds.

Cards released in this expansion reference other cards previously released in the {{dl|List of Pokémon Trading Card Game expansions|EX Series}}. {{TCG ID|Obsidian Flames|Charizard ex|125}}, like {{TCG ID|EX Dragon Frontiers|Charizard ☆ δ |100}} from the {{TCG|EX Dragon Frontiers}} expansion, has the {{TCG|Darkness}} type, and {{TCG ID|Obsidian Flames|Tyranitar ex|66}} has the {{TCG|Lightning}} type like {{TCG ID|EX Dragon Frontiers|Tyranitar ex δ|99}}, also from the EX Dragon Frontiers expansion. Additionally, {{TCG ID|Obsidian Flames|Pidgeot ex|164}} features the Quick Search {{TCG|Ability}}, which first appeared as a {{TCG|Poké-Power}} on {{TCG ID|EX FireRed & LeafGreen|Pidgeot|10}} from the {{TCG|EX FireRed & LeafGreen}} expansion.

This is also the first expansion since the {{dl|List of Pokémon Trading Card Game expansions|Sun & Moon Series}} to feature [[Baby Pokémon]] that do not evolve into {{TCG|Stage 1 Pokémon}}, as it includes {{TCG ID|Obsidian Flames|Cleffa|80}} and {{TCG ID|Obsidian Flames|Bonsly|110}}. These cards have each an attack with no Energy and no [[retreat cost]], like those from the {{dl|List of Pokémon Trading Card Game expansions|HeartGold & SoulSilver Series}}, although unlike previous cards featuring Baby Pokémon, these have Weakness.

The set's Charizard ex can use its Ability to attach {{TCG|Basic Fire Energy}} cards from the player's deck upon Evolution, which can be used to strengthen {{TCG ID|Obsidian Flames|Entei|30}}'s Blaze Ball attack. Another strategy is to use {{TCG ID|Obsidian Flames|Town Store|196}} to find {{TCG|Pokémon Tool card}}s to attach to {{TCG ID|Obsidian Flames|Revavroom ex|156}}, which can have up to four attached simultaneously because of its Ability. {{TCG ID|Obsidian Flames|Lapras|45}}'s Hail attack has a cost of only Colorless Energy, which can allow the player to spread damage to their opponent's Benched Pokémon, then damage those Pokémon further with {{TCG ID|Obsidian Flames|Vespiquen ex|96}}'s Phantom Queen attack. Finally, {{TCG ID|Obsidian Flames|Team Star Grunt|195}}, reprinted in this expansion after its release in the {{TCG|Scarlet & Violet}} expansion, can be used in conjunction with {{TCG ID|Obsidian Flames|Camerupt|32}} to move the opponent's Active Pokémon's Energy to the top of their deck so it can be discarded to increase the power of the Eruption attack.

Obsidian Flames includes cards from the Japanese {{TCG|Ruler of the Black Flame}} expansion, the {{TCG|ex Special Set}} and {{TCG|ex Start Decks}}, remaining cards from the {{TCG|Triplet Beat}}, {{TCG|Snow Hazard}}, and {{TCG|Clay Burst}} sets that were not localized in {{TCG|Paldea Evolved}}, two {{TCG|SV-P Promotional cards|Japanese promo cards}}, and two reprinted cards from the {{TCG|Scarlet & Violet}} expansion. Obsidian Flames contains 15 Pokémon ex, six Tera Pokémon ex, 12 {{TCG|illustration rare}} Pokémon, six {{TCG|special illustration rare}} Pokémon and Supporter cards, 12 {{TCG|ultra rare}} full-art etched Pokémon ex and Supporter cards, and three {{TCG|hyper rare}} gold etched cards.

Although Pokémon cards are typically ordered first by type and then by [[List of Pokémon by National Pokédex number|Pokédex number]], in Obsidian Flames, the {{tcg|ultra rare}} prints of {{TCG ID|Obsidian Flames|Absol ex|214}} (Pokédex number {{p|Absol|#0359}}) and {{TCG ID|Obsidian Flames|Charizard ex|215}} (Pokédex number {{p|Charizard|#0006}}) are numbered 214/197 and 215/197, respectively, despite both being {{ct|Darkness}} Pokémon. However, in the Japanese Ruler of the Black Flame expansion, {{TCG ID|Ruler of the Black Flame|Charizard ex|125}} does come before {{TCG ID|Ruler of the Black Flame|Absol ex|126}}.

Obsidian Flames is available in English, German, French, Italian, Spanish, and Portuguese.

